::ENG3_U06_PT_Q01::MLA.ENG3.C.01
Context: A speaker begins a presentation with a clear claim and relevant evidence.
What skill is being shown?\{
~A. Decoration#Incorrect. The choice is about oral communication, not decoration. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. Organized oral communication#Correct. A clear claim and relevant evidence support an organized presentation. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. Publication export#Incorrect. Exporting is a publication step, not the oral presentation skill here. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Random multimedia use#Incorrect. No multimedia choice is being tested. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q02::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A slide shows a photograph of a courthouse with the caption "public decisions affect families" next to the speaker's claim about civic responsibility.
Why is this effective?\{
~A. It fills space on the slide.#Incorrect. Filling space is not the purpose. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. It replaces evidence.#Incorrect. Media supports evidence but does not replace it. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. It improves audience understanding.#Correct. Labeled, connected media helps the audience follow the message. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. It avoids audience needs.#Incorrect. The choice responds to audience needs. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q03::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student publishes writing for a class LAN page.
What should be checked before publishing?\{
=A. Audience fit, clarity, multimedia support, source information, and conventions#Correct. These checks support publication readiness. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. Only the number of images#Incorrect. Images are not the only publication concern. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Only the background color#Incorrect. Color does not determine writing quality. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Only whether the file is long#Incorrect. Length is not a quality measure. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q04::MLA.ENG3.RS.01
Context: A research summary uses a source but does not explain its connection to the claim.
What is missing?\{
~A. Decoration#Incorrect. The issue is research integration, not decoration. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. Synthesis of the source with the claim#Correct. Research must be connected to the interpretation or purpose. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. A larger font#Incorrect. Font size does not explain source relevance. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. A new unrelated topic#Incorrect. A new topic would weaken focus. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q05::MLA.ENG3.W.04
Context: Feedback says a paragraph is unclear.
What should the student do?\{
~A. Ignore the feedback.#Incorrect. Feedback supports revision. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Change only the color.#Incorrect. Color does not fix unclear writing. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. Revise the wording and organization for clarity.#Correct. Revision should improve clarity and structure. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. Remove all evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is still needed. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q06::MLA.ENG3.V.01
Context: A student uses academic vocabulary in a presentation.
Which use is best?\{
=A. Use precise terms and explain them when needed.#Correct. Academic vocabulary should support audience understanding. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. Use difficult words only to sound advanced.#Incorrect. This can confuse the audience. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Avoid all vocabulary related to the topic.#Incorrect. Precise vocabulary helps communication. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Use terms without connection to the claim.#Incorrect. Vocabulary should support the message. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q07::MLA.ENG3.L.01
Context: A slide caption has spelling errors.
What should happen before submission?\{
~A. Leave the errors because slides are informal.#Incorrect. Presentation text should follow conventions. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. Correct the errors to support clarity and credibility.#Correct. Standard English matters in digital communication. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. Delete the caption even if it explains the image.#Incorrect. A corrected caption may be useful. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Ignore the caption because only images matter.#Incorrect. Captions help explain multimedia. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q08::MLA.ENG3.C.01
Context: A speaker lists evidence but never states a claim.
What is the main problem?\{
=A. The audience may not understand the presentation's focus.#Correct. A claim gives the evidence purpose. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. The presentation has too much audience awareness.#Incorrect. The issue is missing focus. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. The speaker used too many captions.#Incorrect. Captions are not the issue. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. The file is already published.#Incorrect. The context is oral presentation planning. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q09::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A slide chart lists five dates in tiny text; the presentation claim only needs the first and last dates to show change over time.
What should the student do?\{
~A. Keep it small so all details fit.#Incorrect. If the audience cannot read it, it does not help. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. Simplify and enlarge the key information connected to the claim.#Correct. This improves audience experience and understanding. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. Add unrelated graphics.#Incorrect. Unrelated graphics distract. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Remove all labels.#Incorrect. Labels help the audience. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_PT_Q10::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A published summary has no title.
Why is that a problem?\{
~A. Titles are never useful.#Incorrect. Titles guide readers. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. A title replaces all evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is still needed. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. A title helps the audience understand the purpose before reading.#Correct. Audience-aware publication uses clear entry points. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. The page should have only images.#Incorrect. The writing remains important. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}
